Titre$ C R E P I D O T U S 
Com£ Adapté par Paul Bertéa 18-10-2013

1,2 $ All hyphae of fruit body without clamp connections
1,6 $ All hyphae of fruit body with clamp connections

2,3 $ Spores ornamented (sometimes very faintly; oil immersion!)
2,4 $ Spores absolutely smooth, never ornamented

3,0 $ Spores globose to broadly ellipsoid (Q = 1.0 1.3), minutely warty, punctate under the light microscope, 6 8 x 5.5 6.5 µm, with moderately coloured walls; fruitbody red – 8. $ C. cinnabarinus
3,0 $ Spores ellipsoid to oblong subcylindrical (Q = 1.6 2. 1), minutely rugulose to almost smooth, 8.5 12 x 5 6.5 µm, with faintly coloured walls; fruit body white – 17. $ C. versutus

4,5 $ Spores broadly ellipsoid to ellipsoid (Q < 1.7), 7 10 x 5 7 µm, in side view often amygdaliform, with strongly coloured walls; trama of pileus with gelatinous layer; cheilocystidia narrowly utriform to cylindrical
4,0 $ Spores cylindrical, fusoid to pip shaped (Q = 2.1 3.2), 6.5 9.5 x 2.5 3.5 µm,  with weakly coloured walls; trama of pileus without gelatinous layer; cheilocystidia whip like, filiform 16. $ C. epibryus

5,0 $ Fruit body with macroscopically recognizable ferruginous scales; spores 7 10 x 5 7 µm (Q = 1. 3  1.7)  2. $ C. calolepis
5,0 $ Fruit-body without macroscopically recognizable scales, cream, yellowish, pale brown; spores 7 10 x 5 6.5 µm (Q = 1.3 1.7) 1. $ C. mollis

6,7 $ Spores globose to broadly ellipsoid (mean Q < 1.35), echinulate, spiny or punetate under the light microscope
6,13 $ Spores ellipsoid, in side view sometimes amygdaliform (mean Q > 1.4), never echinulate but verruculose or rugulose, warty or smooth under the light microscope

7,8 $ Cheilocystidia with short finger like protuberances, small, < 30 µm long
7,9 $ Cheilocystidia without finger like protuberances, > 30 µm long

8,0 $ Spores 6 8 x 5  7 µm (Q = 1.2 1.5), fruit-body pinkish red 7. $ C. roseoornatus
8,0 $ Spores 5 6.5 x 4.5 6 µm (Q = 1 1.2), fruit body white or yellow when young 6. $ C. carpaticus

9,11 $ Spores 4.5 7.5 x 5 7 µm, distinctly punctate warty, baculate, moderately thick- walled; cheilocystidia never exclusively antler like, mostly clavate or cylindrical, flexuous and often branched; lamellae never pink, crowded
9,10 $ Spores 6 9 x 4.5 7.5 µm, minutely spiny, echinulate, orpunctate warty, rather thin walled; cheilocystidia narrowly lageniform, rarely clavate, branched and often antlerlike; lamellae sometimes pinkish, fairly crowded to distant

10,0 $ Spores 6 9 x 5.5 7.5 µm (Q = 1.1 1.35), echinulate; fruit body < 30 mm, thin; young lamellae often pinkish, subdistant to distant 10. $ C. cesatii
10,0 $ Spores 6 8 x 4.5 6 µm (Q = 1.25 1.5), punctate warty; fruit-body > 30 mm, fleshy; lamellae crowded, never pinkish 9. $ C. macedonicus

11,0 $ Pileus densely covered with orange fulvous scales, never distinctly hygrophanous 4. $ C. crocophyllus
11,12 $ Pileus without scales, often distinctly hygrophanous

12,0 $ Cheilocystidia cylindrical, flexuous, often branched; pileipellis a true trichoderm; pileus rounded flabelliform, semicircular, light orange to melon yellow; young lamellae pale orange 5. $ C. ehrendorferi
12,0 $ Cheilocystidia clavate or lageniform, never flexuous, very rarely mixed with some apically branched ones; pileipellis a cutis with scattered clusters of pileocystidia; pileus often spathuliform, cream to greyish brown; young lamellae cream to clay 3. $ C. applanatus

13,14 $ Spores faintly rugulose or completely smooth; lamellae never pink
13,15 $ Spores distinctly rugulose or verruculose; lamellae occasionally pinkish or yellow when young

14,0 $ Spores completely smooth, 7 9 x 5 6 µm (Q = 13 1.7), with strongly coloured, thick walls; fruit body often fan shaped, terrestrial1  5. $ C. autochthonus
14,0 $ Spores faintly rugulose, 6.5 9.5 x 4.3 6 µm (Q = 1.4 1.7), with moderately to faintly coloured walls; fruit body semicircular, lignicolous 12. $ C. lundellii

15,0 $ Spores ellipsoid to amygdaliform, rugulose, 7.3 10.5 x 5 6.5 µm (Q = 1.4 1.7); cheilocystidia narrowly lageniform 14. $ C. subverrucisporus
15,16 $ Spores elongate to cylindrical (mean Q > 1.7); cheilocystidia cylindrical, flexuous or clavate, obpyriform, sometimes branched

16,0 $ Spores subcylindrical, fusiform, 8 10 x 4 5 µm (Q = 1.8 2.1); cheilocystidia narrowly cylindrical, flexuous, sometimes branched; fruit body either fresh or dried lemon yellow, rarely cream white, margin rarely lobed 13. $ C. luteolus
16,0 $ Spores cylindrical, 6 9 x 3 4.5 µm (Q = 1.6 2.2); fruit body white, margin often lobed to crenate  11. $ C. variabilis
